Single-channel surface electromyography (sEMG) could reduce the cost and complexity of hand-gesture interfaces, but performance commonly degrades when models are tested on unseen subjects. We evaluated five-class hand gesture classification using only the Flexor Carpi Radialis (FCR) channel from 83 quality-screened subjects drawn from 113 recordings. Time-domain (TD) features were classified with multilayer perceptrons (MLPs) and three uncertainty quantification (UQ) methods: Monte Carlo (MC) Dropout, Deep Ensembles, and Evidential Deep Learning (EDL). A raw-window one-dimensional convolutional neural network (1D-CNN) and a Temporal Convolutional Network (TCN) were included as end-to-end comparators. The best within-subject result was obtained by Random Forest at \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
