 expensive stochastic inference methods provide no measurable additional benefit on low-dimensional tabular features.

medicine.text:
Surface electromyography (sEMG) provides a non-invasive measure of neuromuscular activity and is widely studied for powered prostheses, rehabilitation devices, and human–computer interaction [, ]. However, trained classifiers often remain brittle when deployed on new users: performance that is acceptable in within-subject laboratory evaluation can fall sharply for unseen subjects because of variation in electrode placement, skin impedance, muscle anatomy, and motor unit recruitment [–]. Practical adoption is further limited by acquisition complexity. Many high-performing systems rely on dense arrays or carefully selected multi-channel layouts [, , ], which are difficult to translate to low-resource clinical or community prosthetics settings.

Single-channel sEMG is one of the most accessible deployment scenarios, but it also removes the spatial diversity that helps multi-channel systems compensate for subject-specific variability. The result is a difficult combination: low hardware burden, but a large expected gap between within-subject and subject-independent performance. In prosthetic control, this gap is not only an accuracy problem. A model that is confidently wrong can issue an unsafe movement command, whereas an uncertain model can abstain, hold the current state, or request repetition. This makes uncertainty quantification (UQ) particularly relevant for single-channel, subject-independent sEMG, where reliable rejection may be more practical than forcing a prediction for every window. The central question is therefore whether confidence estimates can identify usable segments from otherwise weak cross-subject predictions, and whether probabilistic UQ methods provide any practical advantage over the simpler baseline of plain softmax confidence.

This paper addresses this deployment setting through five contributions: Subject-independent benchmark. We evaluate five-class hand gesture classification with leave-one-subject-out cross-validation (LOSOCV) on an 83-subject quality-screened cohort drawn from 113 recordings, using only the Flexor Carpi Radialis (FCR) channel as a minimum-hardware configuration. We characterise the retained and excluded recordings and show that the exclusion criterion selected recordings with insufficient EMG modulation rather than high DC offset severity. Because a complete demographic and physiological comparison was unavailable, indirect selection bias associated with unmeasured subject characteristics cannot be ruled out.Confidence-based selective prediction and per-class analysis. We show that confidence-based rejection improves per-subject accuracy from 51.1% to 68.3% at 50% coverage and to 83.9% at 30% coverage. Critically, this gain is achieved equally by plain MLP softmax confidence and by MC Dropout, indicating that the improvement arises from the confidence-ordering property of softmax outputs rather than from stochastic inference. MC Dropout and Deep Ensembles add no statistically significant accuracy advantage over the deterministic MLP baseline at full coverage after Holm correction. Per-class analysis further reveals that the accuracy gain is structurally concentrated in the rest class, which is retained at 97.5% coverage with near-perfect accuracy; excluding rest, active-gesture accuracy at 50% coverage is 46.5%. This rest-class dominance effect is a structural property of sEMG datasets with a rest class and should be accounted for in evaluations of selective prediction.Comparative UQ analysis. We compare MC Dropout, Deep Ensembles, and Evidential Deep Learning (EDL) against the deterministic MLP softmax baseline. At full coverage, all three UQ methods are statistically indistinguishable from the baseline. EDL suffers from vacuity collapse on low-dimensional time-domain features and is the only method to degrade under selective prediction. The deterministic MLP achieves the best ECE calibration (0.024), while negative log-likelihood and Brier scores are virtually identical across all methods.TD features versus end-to-end architectures. We compare the time-domain (TD) feature and MLP pipeline against a raw-window one-dimensional convolutional neural network (1D-CNN) and a Temporal Convolutional Network (TCN) []. Both end-to-end architectures showed numerically lower LOSOCV accuracy than the TD+MLP pipeline, though the differences were not statistically significant after Holm correction on the 17 evaluated folds. Loss of amplitude information through the per-sample normalisation required for stable cross-subject end-to-end training is a plausible contributing factor specific to the evaluated preprocessing configuration.External validation. We apply the identical pipeline to NinaPro DB2 [] (40 subjects, Exercise B, one circumferential forearm channel). Classification significantly exceeds chance (p<0.0001), confirming that the TD feature pipeline generalises across recording systems. However, the uncertainty–accuracy correlation (r=+0.047) provides no meaningful signal for identifying hard-to-generalise subjects, indicating that the UQ mechanism does not transfer to this dataset.

Subject-independent benchmark. We evaluate five-class hand gesture classification with leave-one-subject-out cross-validation (LOSOCV) on an 83-subject quality-screened cohort drawn from 113 recordings, using only the Flexor Carpi Radialis (FCR) channel as a minimum-hardware configuration. We characterise the retained and excluded recordings and show that the exclusion criterion selected recordings with insufficient EMG modulation rather than high DC offset severity. Because a complete demographic and physiological comparison was unavailable, indirect selection bias associated with unmeasured subject characteristics cannot be ruled out.

Confidence-based selective prediction and per-class analysis. We show that confidence-based rejection improves per-subject accuracy from 51.1% to 68.3% at 50% coverage and to 83.9% at 30% coverage. Critically, this gain is achieved equally by plain MLP softmax confidence and by MC Dropout, indicating that the improvement arises from the confidence-ordering property of softmax outputs rather than from stochastic inference. MC Dropout and Deep Ensembles add no statistically significant accuracy advantage over the deterministic MLP baseline at full coverage after Holm correction. Per-class analysis further reveals that the accuracy gain is structurally concentrated in the rest class, which is retained at 97.5% coverage with near-perfect accuracy; excluding rest, active-gesture accuracy at 50% coverage is 46.5%. This rest-class dominance effect is a structural property of sEMG datasets with a rest class and should be accounted for in evaluations of selective prediction.

Comparative UQ analysis. We compare MC Dropout, Deep Ensembles, and Evidential Deep Learning (EDL) against the deterministic MLP softmax baseline. At full coverage, all three UQ methods are statistically indistinguishable from the baseline. EDL suffers from vacuity collapse on low-dimensional time-domain features and is the only method to degrade under selective prediction. The deterministic MLP achieves the best ECE calibration (0.024), while negative log-likelihood and Brier scores are virtually identical across all methods.

TD features versus end-to-end architectures. We compare the time-domain (TD) feature and MLP pipeline against a raw-window one-dimensional convolutional neural network (1D-CNN) and a Temporal Convolutional Network (TCN) []. Both end-to-end architectures showed numerically lower LOSOCV accuracy than the TD+MLP pipeline, though the differences were not statistically significant after Holm correction on the 17 evaluated folds. Loss of amplitude information through the per-sample normalisation required for stable cross-subject end-to-end training is a plausible contributing factor specific to the evaluated preprocessing configuration.

External validation. We apply the identical pipeline to NinaPro DB2 [] (40 subjects, Exercise B, one circumferential forearm channel). Classification significantly exceeds chance (p<0.0001), confirming that the TD feature pipeline generalises across recording systems. However, the uncertainty–accuracy correlation (r=+0.047) provides no meaningful signal for identifying hard-to-generalise subjects, indicating that the UQ mechanism does not transfer to this dataset.

TD features such as mean absolute value (MAV), root mean square (RMS), waveform length (WL), zero crossing rate (ZC), and slope sign change (SSC) remain widely used for sEMG classification because they are computationally efficient and perform well in controlled settings [, ]. Window lengths of 150–250 ms with moderate overlap are common because they balance accuracy against controller delay []. Deep learning methods, including convolutional and recurrent networks [–], can improve performance when raw signals or richer spatial representations are available, but they usually require more data and may overfit subject-specific signal structure. Recent work has extended this line through transformer-based sEMG models [, ], single-channel CNN–LSTM processing [], and transfer-learning approaches designed for inter-session or inter-subject generalisation [, ]. Because many studies still rely on within-subject splits, LOSOCV remains important for estimating genuine subject-independent generalisation [, , ].

UQ has become increasingly important in medical artificial intelligence because overconfident predictions can contribute to unsafe decisions []. In sEMG, uncertainty has mainly appeared in rejection-based control, where low-confidence windows are withheld [], but formal probabilistic calibration is less developed. MC Dropout approximates Bayesian inference by retaining dropout during inference and averaging stochastic predictions []; it has shown useful uncertainty behaviour in electrocardiogram (ECG) and motor-imagery brain-computer interface (BCI) tasks [, ]. Deep Ensembles average independently trained networks and often provide strong calibration across benchmarks [, ], but require multiple training runs. EDL places a Dirichlet prior over class probabilities and estimates vacuity in a single forward pass []. Although EDL has shown promise in high-dimensional medical imaging [], recent analyses have questioned whether standard EDL objectives reliably quantify epistemic uncertainty across settings [, ]. Its behaviour on low-dimensional tabular biomedical features therefore remains an important empirical question.

This section describes the dataset and quality screening procedure, the preprocessing and feature extraction pipeline, the classifier architectures and UQ methods evaluated, the selective prediction and calibration frameworks, the statistical analysis approach, and the evaluation protocols for both the primary YTU dataset and the NinaPro DB2 external validation.

The dataset comprises sEMG recordings from 113 healthy subjects (mean age 23.4± 3.1 years) collected at Yıldız Technical University using a BIOPAC MP36 amplifier at 10 kHz sampling rate. Subjects performed five hand movements: Hand Free (rest), Fist, Wrist Flexion, Wrist Extension, and Hand Stretching. Each movement was held for approximately 4 seconds, with rest periods between movements. Three channels were recorded; only the FCR channel was used throughout this study, representing a single-electrode low-resource acquisition scenario.

Raw signals were segmented using predefined sample indices with 5000 samples trimmed from each end to remove transition artefacts, yielding 4,000 samples (4 seconds at effective 1000 Hz after 10×  downsampling) per movement per subject. The window count arithmetic confirms this value: \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
