A key insight is that DC offset does not necessarily invalidate a recording. Muscle activation rides on top of the offset floor, so EMG variation is still present in the signal; it is simply elevated. To measure this variation independently of the offset, quality was assessed using STD contrast: the ratio of the active-segment STD to the rest-segment STD after DC removal. This metric is insensitive to absolute signal level and directly captures the degree to which the EMG modulates during movement.

Subjects were assigned to quality tiers (GOOD, REVIEW, POOR, EXCLUDE) based on STD contrast and spectral characteristics. For all classification experiments, 83 subjects (GOOD: 66, REVIEW: 17) were retained, discarding 30 subjects with fewer than 1.5×  STD contrast across all active movements. This screening step defines the scope of the benchmark: these 83 subjects represent the regime where single-channel FCR acquisition is viable.

Each movement segment underwent the following preprocessing steps. First, DC removal: the segment mean was subtracted to eliminate electrode offset. No amplitude normalisation was applied at this stage, as absolute amplitude carries discriminative information between rest and active states that would be destroyed by normalisation (see Section ). Second, bandpass filtering: a 4th-order zero-phase Butterworth filter with a 20–150 Hz passband was applied via scipy.signal.filtfilt, attenuating movement artefact below 20 Hz and high-frequency noise above 150 Hz while preserving the sEMG band.

