To benchmark the TD+MLP pipeline against an end-to-end deep learning approach, we evaluated a one-dimensional convolutional neural network (1D-CNN) operating directly on raw 200-sample windows. The architecture consists of three convolutional blocks (32, 64, and 128 filters with kernel sizes 9, 7, and 5 respectively), each followed by batch normalisation and ReLU activation, with a final global average pooling layer. The classification head comprises a 128-to-64 linear layer with ReLU and dropout (p=0.3), followed by a 64-to-K output layer. Because raw window amplitudes vary substantially across subjects, the 1D-CNN applies per-sample amplitude normalisation (zero mean, unit variance) prior to training and inference. This normalisation is necessary for stable CNN training but, as discussed in Section , discards the amplitude information that is discriminative in this dataset.

We additionally evaluated a Temporal Convolutional Network (TCN) [] to provide a dilated temporal deep learning comparator beyond the 1D-CNN. The architecture comprises four residual blocks with dilated convolutions (dilations 1, 2, 4, and 8), each block using 64 filters with kernel size 3, batch normalisation, ReLU activations, and dropout (p=0.3). Residual connections with a pointwise projection where input and output channels differ connect each block. Global average pooling followed by a linear classifier head maps the temporal features to K class scores. Like the 1D-CNN, the TCN applies per-sample amplitude normalisation before training and inference.

