The selective prediction framework operationalises confidence-based rejection for deployment: instead of always returning a prediction, the classifier may abstain on windows for which confidence is below a threshold. In a prosthetics context, abstention means the device holds its current position or signals the user to repeat the gesture, which is a safer outcome than an incorrect movement command. Coverage curves are computed separately per subject: for each held-out subject, their windows are ranked from most to least confident, the top \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
