For each subject, windows from each movement class are split 80/20 (train/test), with the split applied independently per class to ensure all five movement classes appear in both partitions. Models are trained and tested on the same subject. This is the standard protocol used in the majority of published sEMG classification studies [, ], and serves here as the upper-bound reference point.

In each of 83 folds, one subject is held out as the test set and the model is trained on the remaining 82 subjects. No data from the test subject is seen during training or feature normalisation, making this a true subject-independent evaluation.

The 1D-CNN and TCN were evaluated on a stratified subset of 17 folds (every fifth subject) due to the substantially higher per-fold training cost of convolutional architectures. All MLP-based methods were evaluated across all 83 folds. All experiments were implemented in PyTorch (GPU: NVIDIA A100) and scikit-learn. XGBoost used CUDA device acceleration. Each LOSOCV fold for the MLP-based pipeline required approximately 39 seconds (1 MLP baseline model, 1 MC Dropout model, 3 Ensemble models, 1 EDL model), yielding a total runtime of approximately 54 minutes for the full 83-fold evaluation. Inference cost per classification window is under 1 ms for EDL (single forward pass), under 10 ms for Deep Ensembles (3 passes), and under 10 ms for MC Dropout (30 stochastic passes), all well within the 100–300 ms latency budget of real-time prosthetic controllers [, ]. The 1D-CNN required approximately 410 seconds per fold, and the TCN approximately 430 seconds per fold.

To evaluate whether the pipeline generalises to an independent dataset, we applied the identical preprocessing and classification pipeline to NinaPro DB2 [], a publicly available sEMG database comprising recordings from 40 intact subjects using 12 Delsys Trigno electrodes sampled at 2 kHz. We used Exercise B, which contains 17 finger and wrist movements. Four movements were mapped onto our gesture set: label 6 (fingers flexed in fist), label 11 (wrist flexion), label 12 (wrist extension), and label 5 (abduction of all fingers, analogous to hand stretching), plus label 0 (rest). A single electrode was selected (channel index 7, 0-indexed). This channel is one of the eight electrodes equally spaced around the forearm at the radiohumeral-joint level in the standard NinaPro DB2 arrangement, rather than an anatomically targeted FCR electrode. It is therefore described throughout as a single circumferential forearm channel [].

Preprocessing followed the YTU pipeline exactly: the signal was first decimated from 2 kHz to 1 kHz, then demeaned, then bandpass-filtered (20–150 Hz, 4th-order Butterworth, zero-phase). The same nine TD features were extracted with the same 200-sample window and 100-sample step. Because NinaPro labels every inter-movement rest period with label 0, the rest class was substantially over-represented in the raw windowed data (approximately 15.6 times more rest windows than the mean active-class count). To create a fair five-class comparison analogous to the YTU setup, rest windows were randomly subsampled per subject to match the mean active-class window count for that subject. Full LOSOCV was performed with MC Dropout only, using the same inference procedure applied to the YTU dataset.

Results are reported in four parts: signal quality screening and cohort characterisation; baseline within-subject and LOSOCV classification performance across all six classifiers; UQ results including selective prediction, calibration, subject-level uncertainty, per-class analysis, and statistical validation; and external validation on NinaPro DB2.

