Within-subject, RF and LDA achieve comparable performance (73.3% and 73.2% respectively), with overlapping 95% confidence intervals; both results are within the broad range reported for controlled sEMG gesture classification, although direct numerical comparison is limited by differences in channels, gesture sets, and validation protocols [, ]. The MLP (71.0%) and XGBoost (69.9%) perform competitively. The reversal of classifier rankings between WS and LOSOCV is notable: LDA performs comparably to RF under WS but ranks last under LOSOCV, while MLP leads under LOSOCV. This reflects the bias–variance trade-off. LDA has low variance relative to the small per-subject training set (approximately 130 windows per class in the 80/20 split), making it stable within a single subject’s feature distribution. The MLP, with substantially more parameters, has higher variance on a single subject’s data but benefits from the full 82-subject training pool in LOSOCV and can learn population-level decision boundaries that LDA cannot represent.

The 1D-CNN achieved 52.5% within-subject accuracy and the TCN only 34.9%, both substantially below the TD classifiers despite being evaluated on the same 83-subject cohort. These results reflect the limited per-subject training data available in the 80/20 WS split (approximately 650 windows total), which is insufficient to train morphology-sensitive convolutional models reliably.

The mean accuracy gap between WS and LOSOCV across all four TD classifiers is approximately 22 pp (LDA: 26.0 pp, RF: 24.0 pp, XGBoost: 20.2 pp, MLP: 19.9 pp). Rather than attempting to close this gap purely through model design, the subsequent analysis asks whether confidence estimates can identify at inference time which windows the cross-subject model can reliably classify.

