Figure  shows per-subject mean accuracy as a function of coverage, with 95-percentile CIs across subjects. MC Dropout, Deep Ensembles, and the deterministic MLP softmax all exhibit consistent monotonic improvement as coverage decreases, and their curves are nearly indistinguishable from one another. At per-subject 50% coverage, MC Dropout and the MLP baseline both achieve 68.3% mean accuracy (gains of +17.0 pp and +17.2 pp respectively); Deep Ensembles achieve 68.1% (+16.8 pp). At 30% coverage, all three methods reach approximately 83–84% accuracy.

The similarity between MC Dropout and plain MLP softmax in selective prediction indicates that the gain arises from the confidence-ordering property of softmax probability outputs, not from stochastic inference. The 30 forward passes required by MC Dropout provide no additional coverage-accuracy advantage over the single deterministic pass.

EDL shows the opposite trend: accuracy decreases from full to reduced coverage (51.0% to 39.5% at 50% per-subject coverage). This occurs because EDL’s vacuity scores do not order windows by correctness; the model is disproportionately confident on windows it classifies incorrectly. Applying a confidence threshold therefore actively selects for incorrect predictions, inverting the intended behaviour of selective prediction.

