LOSOCV accuracy on NinaPro DB2 (34.1%) is lower than on the YTU dataset (51.3%). This is expected: NinaPro uses a standardised circumferential electrode ring, and the selected channel is not anatomically targeted to FCR with the subject-specific precision of the dedicated FCR electrode in the YTU dataset. Classification significantly exceeds chance across all 40 subjects (p<0.0001), confirming that the TD feature pipeline partially generalises across recording systems.

However, the per-subject uncertainty–accuracy correlation is r=+0.047, indicating no meaningful relationship between uncertainty and subject-level difficulty on this dataset. The mean per-subject uncertainty across all NinaPro subjects was approximately 0.92 (on a [0, 1] scale), indicating that the model operates near maximum entropy throughout. Under these conditions, the confidence ordering carries little discriminative information, and selective prediction provides only a small gain: +2.6 pp at 50% coverage and +6.1 pp at 30% coverage. These results indicate that the UQ mechanism does not generalise to NinaPro DB2 under this configuration.

The results establish two main findings. First, confidence-based rejection consistently improves accuracy from 51.1% to 68.3% at 50% per-subject coverage on the YTU dataset, but this improvement is achieved equally by plain MLP softmax confidence and by MC Dropout. Second, the UQ mechanism does not transfer to NinaPro DB2, where the model operates near maximum entropy and uncertainty carries no information about per-subject difficulty. The following subsections interpret these findings in the context of prior work and assess the practical implications.

The within-subject result of 73.3% with nine TD features from a single FCR channel is consistent with the feasibility of recent single-channel sEMG classification studies, although reported accuracies are not directly comparable across different gesture sets and validation protocols [, ]. Three factors contribute to this performance level. First, the quality filtering step removed 30 subjects whose signals lacked sufficient STD contrast, concentrating the evaluated cohort on recordings with clean EMG modulation. Second, preserving absolute amplitude through DC-only removal was critical: amplitude normalisation substantially reduced within-subject accuracy in sensitivity experiments because it removes the absolute scale carried by MAV, RMS, and IEMG, weakening the primary discriminative cue between rest and active states. Third, LDA’s low variance on small per-subject training sets makes it well-suited to the 80/20 single-subject split, where only approximately 130 windows per class are available for training.

The mean 22 pp gap between WS (73%) and LOSOCV (51%) accuracy is consistent with the broader literature showing that cross-user myoelectric generalisation remains substantially harder than within-user evaluation [, , , ]. Under single-channel constraints, this gap is expected to be large for two reasons. First, the model cannot exploit spatial diversity across electrode sites to compensate for subject-specific motor unit recruitment patterns. Second, the FCR electrode is primarily sensitive to wrist flexion and fist movements. Wrist extension recruits the antagonist extensor muscles, which are largely silent in FCR recordings, creating inherent ambiguity between rest and extension states that varies across individuals depending on co-contraction habits and anatomy. Hand stretching presents a similar challenge. These class-specific limitations motivate extending the framework to all three recorded channels in future work.

The +17.0 pp accuracy gain at 50% per-subject coverage is the primary practical finding of this work. However, the critical result is that this gain is achieved equally by the deterministic MLP softmax baseline and by MC Dropout. The per-subject accuracy curves in Fig.  are nearly indistinguishable between these methods, and the fixed-threshold analysis confirms that the gain is achievable without access to test-subject labels.

This outcome arises mechanistically from the structure of the feature space. Rest windows have systematically higher amplitude than active-movement windows in single-channel FCR recordings, and the MLP learns correspondingly high-confidence softmax outputs for rest-class predictions. At 50% per-subject coverage, nearly all rest windows are retained (97.5%) while active-gesture windows are substantially pruned (Table ). The headline accuracy figure therefore reflects near-perfect rest classification on a large proportion of the retained set, rather than improved discrimination among active gestures. The per-class analysis in Section  makes this concrete: excluding rest, active-gesture accuracy rises only from 39.5% to 46.5% at 50% coverage.

From a deployment perspective, the finding that plain softmax confidence suffices for this rejection task is practically relevant: the 30 forward passes required by MC Dropout, which run in under 10 ms on the tested hardware, are nonetheless unnecessary overhead for a task achievable with a single forward pass. In settings where inference budget is tighter: embedded microcontrollers or battery-powered prosthetic units: this distinction matters.

The negative correlation between per-subject MC Dropout uncertainty and LOSOCV accuracy is r=-0.142. The uncertainty signal is not a reliable pre-deployment screening tool at this scale.

The EDL vacuity collapse observed here is consistent with recent analyses questioning the reliability of epistemic uncertainty produced by standard EDL objectives and showing strong sensitivity to training settings [, ]. In our setting, on low-dimensional inputs the Softplus output head can satisfy the training loss by learning to produce large evidence values for all classes simultaneously. This drives the Dirichlet concentration high and vacuity low across the board. The KL annealing term is intended to penalise this behaviour, but with a 9-dimensional feature space the network has sufficient capacity to overfit the evidence head before the annealing weight reaches its full value.

Potential remedies include using a stronger KL weight (λ >0.01), normalising evidence by a learnable temperature parameter, or using a higher-dimensional feature space such as spectrogram inputs where the evidence head cannot as easily produce uniformly large values. Whether EDL recovers on raw time-series inputs processed by a 1D-CNN is an important open question.

Both the 1D-CNN (LOSOCV 44.6%) and the TCN (LOSOCV 44.3%) perform below the MLP baseline (51.1%) under subject-independent evaluation, though the differences are not significant after Holm correction across the 17 sampled folds. The comparison is specific to the amplitude-normalised preprocessing configuration required for stable cross-subject end-to-end training. End-to-end training on raw sEMG windows requires per-sample amplitude normalisation to ensure stable gradient flow across subjects with different signal magnitudes. However, absolute amplitude is a plausible primary cue separating rest from active states in single-channel FCR recordings: the rest segment is systematically lower in amplitude than any active movement, and this information is explicitly encoded by MAV, RMS, and IEMG. Per-sample normalisation to zero mean and unit variance removes this potentially discriminative cue, which may force the model to rely more heavily on waveform morphology, a signal property that is far more subject-specific and harder to generalise.

The TD+MLP pipeline avoids this trade-off by design. DC removal preserves relative amplitude relationships within a subject while eliminating electrode offset, and the nine TD features encode amplitude explicitly. The TCN, despite using dilated convolutions with a larger receptive field than the 1D-CNN, does not escape this constraint because it applies the same per-sample amplitude normalisation before training. These observations suggest that, under the evaluated preprocessing configuration and for single-channel sEMG with a hand-free rest class, the TD+MLP pipeline may be better-suited for cross-subject deployment because it retains amplitude information that the normalisation step removes from end-to-end inputs.

Regarding transformer-based architectures, a standard transformer requires a meaningful token sequence, whereas the present 9-dimensional TD feature vector has no natural sequential structure. Recent transformer-based sEMG systems have therefore operated on multichannel or high-density temporal representations [, ]. Likewise, recent transfer-learning studies demonstrate promising inter-session and inter-subject adaptation [, ], but incorporating a calibration or adaptation stage would address a different deployment protocol from the calibration-free LOSOCV benchmark evaluated here. Both directions remain open for future work.

The NinaPro DB2 external validation confirms that the TD feature pipeline generalises across recording systems: accuracy significantly exceeds chance across all 40 subjects (p<0.0001). However, the UQ mechanism does not generalise to this dataset. The mean per-subject uncertainty of approximately 0.92 indicates that the model operates near maximum predictive entropy throughout: every NinaPro subject appears equally uncertain, regardless of how well or poorly the model performs on that subject. As a result, the uncertainty signal carries no discriminative information about subject-level difficulty (r=+0.047), and selective prediction provides only small gains (+2.6 pp at 50% coverage, +6.1 pp at 30% coverage).

The high uniform uncertainty is mechanistically consistent with the dataset difference: the YTU pipeline was trained and evaluated on FCR recordings, while the NinaPro channel (index 7) is a circumferential forearm electrode not specifically targeting FCR. The resulting signal distribution is sufficiently different from the training distribution that the model cannot meaningfully discriminate confident from uncertain predictions. This is not a failure of the classification pipeline per se: above-chance accuracy confirms that the TD features capture some generalisable information: but rather a failure of the UQ signal to remain calibrated under this degree of distributional shift.

These results highlight an important limitation of confidence-based abstention in subject-independent deployment: the mechanism is only useful when the model’s confidence estimates correlate with actual prediction difficulty. Under severe distributional mismatch, high uniform uncertainty renders abstention uninformative.

Several limitations should be noted. First, only one EMG channel was used; the full three-channel recording would provide extensor muscle information and is expected to substantially reduce the within-subject/cross-subject gap. Second, the dataset consists entirely of healthy subjects; performance on amputee populations cannot be inferred from the present results. Third, all experiments used offline classification with pre-segmented windows; real-time deployment requires online segmentation, streaming feature extraction, and potentially online adaptation, none of which are evaluated here. Fourth, the five gesture classes evaluated represent a limited subset of the movement vocabulary required for full prosthetic control. Fifth, the present study uses single-session recordings, whereas practical use introduces electrode displacement, muscle fatigue, and long-term signal variability that future work should address through online adaptation mechanisms [, , ]. Finally, the selective prediction gain on the YTU dataset is concentrated in the rest class; a deployment system that relies primarily on this gain should be evaluated specifically on active-gesture windows to determine practical utility.

This study evaluated uncertainty-aware single-channel sEMG classification for five-class hand gesture recognition under LOSOCV on 83 quality-screened subjects, and validated the pipeline on NinaPro DB2.

