The deterministic MLP achieved the best ECE calibration (0.024), while NLL and Brier scores were virtually identical across all methods, indicating that probabilistic UQ adds no advantage in terms of proper scoring rules on these low-dimensional tabular features. EDL failed due to vacuity collapse (ECE=0.341), providing a clear empirical negative result that helps delimit the applicability of evidential methods to low-dimensional tabular sEMG data. Both 1D-CNN and TCN showed numerically lower LOSOCV accuracy than the TD+MLP pipeline, a difference that was not statistically significant after Holm correction and is specific to the amplitude-normalised preprocessing configuration evaluated here.

External validation on NinaPro DB2 confirmed above-chance classification (p < 0.0001) but showed no meaningful uncertainty–accuracy correlation (r = +0.047), indicating that the UQ mechanism does not generalise to this dataset under the observed degree of distributional shift.

The principal practical implication is that confidence-based rejection is viable for low-resource single-channel sEMG deployment, but that plain softmax confidence is sufficient. Future work should evaluate whether more powerful UQ methods, richer feature representations, or domain adaptation can restore the discriminative value of uncertainty signals under cross-dataset deployment.

psychology.text:
Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) is among the most prevalent neurodevelopmental disorders in childhood and is primarily characterized by three core behavioral symptoms—inattention, hyperactivity, and impulsivity—that are developmentally inappropriate relative to age-matched peers. Approximately 60% of individuals diagnosed with ADHD in childhood continue to exhibit symptoms into adulthood, frequently accompanied by comorbid conditions and an elevated risk of multidomain functional impairment. The global prevalence of ADHD among school-age children and adolescents is estimated at 7.2%. A 2022 epidemiological survey reported a prevalence of 6.4% among school-attending children aged 6 to 16 years in China. According to the most recent Global Burden of Disease study, the disease burden attributable to ADHD among children in China has increased steadily from 1990 to 2021, commonly resulting in functional impairments such as academic underachievement and difficulties in interpersonal relationships.

The clinical presentation of ADHD extends beyond its core behavioral symptoms. Emotional symptoms, including irritability and emotional lability, represent prominent and readily observable manifestations in children experiencing emotional dysregulation., Neurobiological evidence provides mechanistic support for this association. Children with ADHD have been demonstrated to exhibit functional network abnormalities in brain regions implicated in emotional processing, such as the superior medial orbitofrontal cortex, which may represent a biological substrate underlying the development of emotional symptoms. Interventional studies further support this perspective. Transcranial direct current stimulation targeting emotion-related neural circuits has been associated with improved performance on emotion-related tasks in children with ADHD, and working memory training has been reported to enhance emotional regulation in this population.,

Although emotional symptoms have commonly been conceptualized as comorbid features or secondary outcomes of ADHD, their potential role as mediators within the core pathological pathway linking behavioral symptoms to functional impairment has not been comprehensively examined. Specifically, whether emotional symptoms mediate the association between core behavioral symptoms and functional impairment—and the magnitude of this effect requires rigorous evaluation in large-sample empirical studies.

The present study employed structural equation modeling (SEM) to systematically assess the mediating role of emotional symptoms in the relationship between core ADHD symptoms and social impairment and to quantify the magnitude of this mediating effect. The findings are intended to provide an empirical foundation for the development of a dual-domain “behavior–emotion” nursing intervention framework for children with ADHD.

A cross-sectional survey design with convenience sampling was employed. Children diagnosed with ADHD who attended Shunyi Maternal and Children’s Hospital of Beijing Children’s Hospital between January and November 2024 were recruited.

The inclusion criteria were as follows: (1) age 6–17 years; (2) fulfillment of the diagnostic criteria for ADHD according to the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, Fifth Edition (DSM-5); (3) no prior pharmacological treatment for any neurodevelopmental disorder, including ADHD, tic disorder, autism spectrum disorder (ASD), or related conditions, before enrollment; and (4) written informed consent provided by the legal guardian.

The exclusion criteria were as follows: (1) comorbid severe physical illness, epilepsy, intellectual disability (IQ < 70), or other major psychiatric disorders (eg, schizophrenia or bipolar disorder); (2) visual or auditory impairment, or cognitive capacity insufficient to complete the required assessments; and (3) incomplete questionnaire responses, patterned responding, or other evidence of invalid data, which were classified as invalid questionnaires.

According to general SEM principles, the recommended sample size is 10–20 times the number of observed variables. The present study included 9 general demographic variables and 9 scale-derived observed variables, totaling 18 variables, resulting in an estimated required sample size of 180–360 participants. Assuming an anticipated invalid questionnaire rate of 20%, the adjusted required sample size was 216–432 cases. Ultimately, 403 valid cases were included in the final analysis. The study protocol was approved by the Ethics Committee of Shunyi Maternal and Children’s Hospital of Beijing Children’s Hospital (Approval No. 2023-021-01). All assessments were conducted after written informed consent had been obtained from the legal guardians of the participating children.

The general demographic questionnaire was developed by the research team based on relevant literature and consultation with two pediatric nursing specialists and one child psychiatry specialist. The instrument collected information on the child’s sex, age, only-child status, maternal reproductive history, caregiver educational level, and caregiver monthly income. The questionnaire was completed by the legal guardian.

The Chinese version of the SNAP-IV has demonstrated satisfactory reliability and validity., The scale is designed to assess behavioral symptoms and their severity in children aged 6–18 years diagnosed with ADHD and consists of 26 items across three dimensions: inattention, hyperactivity–impulsivity, and oppositional defiant behavior. Parents rate their child’s behavior over the preceding six months. Completion typically requires approximately 10 minutes. Items are scored on a 4-point Likert scale (0 = never, 1 = sometimes, 2 = often, 3 = always). Dimension scores are calculated by summing the corresponding item scores and dividing by the number of items to obtain a mean score. The severity classification is defined as follows: 0–1.0 = normal, 1.1–1.5 = mild, 1.6–2.0 = moderate, and 2.1–3.0 = severe. Higher scores indicate greater symptom severity. In the present study, the Cronbach’s α coefficient for the total scale was 0.95. The coefficients for the inattention, hyperactivity–impulsivity, and oppositional defiant behavior dimensions were 0.90, 0.89, and 0.88, respectively.

The Strengths and Difficulties Questionnaire (SDQ) was originally developed by Goodman, and the Chinese version was revised by Kou et al,, It is used to screen for emotional and behavioral problems in children aged 4–16 years. In this study, the Emotional Symptoms subscale was used as the primary mediating variable. The Emotional Symptoms subscale comprises five items (eg, “Often complains of headaches, stomachaches, or sickness”) rated on a 3-point Likert scale (0 = not true, 1 = somewhat true, 2 = certainly true). Parents complete the subscale based on their child’s behavior over the preceding six months. Higher scores indicate more pronounced emotional symptoms. In the present study, the Cronbach’s α coefficient for this subscale was 0.82. The parent version cutoff scores are defined as follows: 0–3 = normal, 4 = borderline, and 5–10 = abnormal. These cutoff values are intended for screening purposes and do not constitute diagnostic criteria.

The WFIRS-P is a functional assessment instrument developed specifically for ADHD to evaluate the extent to which symptoms and associated behavioral and emotional problems affect functional domains. The scale comprises six dimensions—family, learning/school, daily living skills, self-concept, social activities, and risk-taking activities—with a total of 50 items. Items are rated on a 4-point Likert scale (0–3) and completed by parents. Administration typically requires approximately 15 minutes. The maximum scores for the six dimensions are 30, 30, 30, 9, 21, and 30, respectively, yielding a total possible score range of 0–150. Higher scores indicate greater functional impairment. The clinical threshold for functional impairment is defined as a mean scale score > 1.5, or the presence of at least two items scored 2 or at least one item scored 3 within any single dimension. The scale demonstrates satisfactory reliability. In the present study, the Cronbach’s α coefficient for the total scale was 0.92, and the coefficients for the individual dimensions ranged from 0.70 to 0.89.

Data collection was conducted by research nurses who had received standardized training. After written informed consent was obtained from the legal guardians, the research nurses instructed the guardians to complete the general demographic questionnaire, SNAP-IV, SDQ, and WFIRS-P using the hospital’s standardized psychological assessment system. An electronic data capture system was implemented, with all items designated as mandatory fields to prevent item-level missing data. Upon completion, a designated staff member performed data verification for quality control.

Questionnaires were classified as invalid and excluded under the following conditions: (1) evidence of patterned responding (eg, 10 or more consecutive identical responses or other regular response patterns); and (2) internal logical inconsistencies within a scale (eg, contradictory responses between positively and negatively scored items on the SDQ). A total of 481 electronic questionnaires were distributed. Seventy-eight questionnaires were excluded as invalid according to the predefined criteria, resulting in 403 valid questionnaires and an effective response rate of 83.8%.

