To further clarify the underlying mechanism, an SEM was constructed (). Model fit indices indicated an acceptable fit (χ2/df = 3.458, RMSEA = 0.078, CFI = 0.99). Bootstrap analysis () demonstrated that the total effect of ADHD core behavioral symptoms on social impairment was 1.13, comprising a direct effect of 0.87 and an indirect effect of 0.25 mediated by emotional symptoms. The indirect effect accounted for 22.69% of the total effect, and the 95% confidence interval excluded 0, indicating statistical significance. Within the hypothesized mediating pathway of “ADHD core behaviors → emotional symptoms → social impairment”, emotional symptoms functioned as a partial mediator. These findings indicate that social impairment is attributable not only to the direct effects of core behavioral symptoms but also, to a substantial extent (22.69%), to indirect effects transmitted through emotional symptoms.
