In the present study, the severity of ADHD core behavioral symptoms (SNAP-IV item mean score: 1.45 ± 0.87) was within the mild-to-moderate range, indicating a clinically meaningful level of behavioral disturbance and the need for structured behavioral management within nursing practice. The mean emotional symptoms score (4.50 ± 3.42) approached the borderline threshold, indicating that a substantial proportion of children exhibited identifiable emotional difficulties. The level of social impairment (WFIRS-P item mean score: 1.24 ± 1.07) was most pronounced in the domains of family functioning, learning/school performance, and daily living skills, highlighting the multidimensional impact of ADHD on daily social functioning.

Compared with the findings reported by Xue et al, emotional symptom scores were more prominent in the present sample. This discrepancy may be attributable to recruitment from a psychiatric specialty outpatient clinic, where children may present with more complex or severe symptom profiles. These findings indicate that an exclusive emphasis on behavioral symptoms during nursing assessment may lead to under recognition of broader functional challenges and associated risks.

Female children with ADHD demonstrated significantly greater social impairment than male children. This finding may be related to the higher prevalence of predominantly inattentive symptom presentations among females, greater concealment of symptoms, and a later age at diagnosis, which may contribute to delayed intervention and cumulative functional impairment over time. Children aged ≥ 13 years exhibited the highest level of functional impairment, indicating that adolescence represents a critical period of vulnerability for functional deterioration in ADHD. Increasing academic demands, more complex peer relationships, and heightened expectations for self-management may impose substantial challenges on executive functioning and emotional regulation capacities.

Greater functional impairment observed among children from multi-child families may be associated with the allocation of parental attention and emotional resources within the household. Collectively, these findings indicate that nursing assessments should prioritize the identification of high-risk subgroups, including female children, adolescents, and those from multi-child families and that stratified intervention strategies incorporating enhanced family support should be implemented.

Significant positive correlations were identified among ADHD core behavioral symptoms, emotional symptoms, and social impairment (all p < 0.01). Emotional symptoms demonstrated a strength of association with social impairment comparable to that of core behavioral symptoms. The strong positive correlation between ADHD core behavioral symptoms and emotional symptoms provides an empirical basis for examining the mediating role of emotional symptoms and supports the theoretical perspective that executive function deficits and impairments in emotional regulation interact to contribute to functional impairment.

In the absence of effective emotional regulation strategies, children with ADHD may exhibit heightened reactivity to frustration and increased vulnerability to negative emotional states in daily contexts. These findings underscore the importance of incorporating emotional dimensions into comprehensive ADHD assessment frameworks.

Structural equation modeling confirmed that emotional symptoms partially mediated the association between ADHD core behavioral symptoms and social impairment, with the indirect effect accounting for 22.69% of the total effect. This mediating pathway is supported by neurobiological and interventional evidence. Estaji et al reported that transcranial direct current stimulation targeting emotion-related regions of the prefrontal cortex significantly improved performance on emotional tasks in children with ADHD. Zhao et al demonstrated that working memory training enhanced emotional regulation capacity in this population. Such findings provide convergent support for the identification of emotional symptoms as a modifiable mediating factor.

The neurodevelopmental characteristics of ADHD, manifested as core behavioral symptoms, contribute directly to functional impairment. In addition, a substantial proportion (22.69%) of functional impairment appears to be indirectly transmitted through emotional dysregulation, which further disrupts social functioning. This mechanism may partially explain the high comorbidity between ADHD and internalizing problems.

These findings have practical implications for nursing care. First, standardized emotional symptom assessment tools should be incorporated into routine evaluations of children presenting with ADHD core behavioral symptoms. Second, nursing interventions should integrate evidence-based emotional regulation strategies. Third, given the bidirectional relationship between family functioning and children’s emotional symptoms, structured parent education and guidance on parent–child emotion management should be established as central components of comprehensive intervention programs.

Based on the mediating pathway identified in this study, a dual-track nursing model for ADHD functional recovery is proposed. One track focuses on the direct management of core behavioral symptoms, whereas the other aims to interrupt the “behavior → emotion → function” transmission pathway through structured emotional regulation interventions. This framework extends nursing practice beyond conventional symptom-focused management toward an integrated emotion–function rehabilitation approach and provides a theoretical foundation for the standardized development of intervention protocols. The systematic incorporation of emotional symptom assessment and targeted emotion regulation strategies into routine nursing workflows may facilitate improvements in overall social functioning among children with ADHD.

However, the findings of this study are derived from a cross-sectional design and rely primarily on parent-reported measures. Future research should employ longitudinal designs to further clarify the temporal and causal relationships within the proposed mediating pathway. In addition, standardized nursing intervention protocols based on this framework should be developed and evaluated through randomized controlled trials to generate higher-level evidence to support clinical practice.

As of September 2026, the term “fake news” has appeared in the title or abstract of over 2,000 publications this year, and we can expect this figure to exceed 3,000 as it has every year since 2021 (data from https://www.dimensions.ai/). Despite the avid scholarly interest in fake news, precisely what is meant by the term remains unclear. This may seem surprising given that we have a dictionary definition of fake news—“false stories that appear to be news, spread on the internet or using other media, usually created to influence political views or as a joke” ()—which aligns with how fake news is typically described in the scientific literature (e.g., Lazer et al., ; Tandoc et al., ). Nevertheless, operationalizations of the term vary widely across studies.

One issue lies in determining what is and is not false, and thus what constitutes false stories. Indeed, what qualifies information as false (Uscinski et al., )? Does there have to be demonstrable evidence of its falsehood, does it have to contradict the scientific consensus, or does it simply have to be subjectively objectionable? In practice, all three criteria have been used to label content as false, despite each of them representing different standards of judgment. Consider the following three statements: (a) the 2024 Southport stabbings were carried out by a Muslim asylum seeker; (b) human-caused climate change is not happening; and (c) COVID-19 originated in a laboratory. Statement (a) is demonstrably false; the attacker was a British citizen born in Cardiff, Wales, to Christian parents (Casciani & Holt, ). Statement (b) contradicts the scientific consensus; while the Earth’s climate is a complex system with natural variability and long-term trends that make it difficult to isolate the impact of specific factors, the overwhelming scientific consensus is that human activities are driving global warming (NASA Science, ). Statement (c) remains inconclusive; although much of the scientific evidence suggests COVID-19 had a natural, zoonotic origin, circumstantial evidence exists that supports a laboratory origin (Yoo, ). Despite each claim’s distinct epistemic status, they have all at some point been labeled as false by the commentariat (Fraser, ; Lewis, ; Spring, ; Uscinski, ).

Falsity is central to the definition of fake news. Therefore, the conceptual vagueness surrounding falsity renders the notion of fake news equally ambiguous, and this is reflected in the scientific literature. Tandoc et al. () reviewed 34 papers published between 2003 and 2017 that used the term “fake news” and found six typologies: news satire, news parody, fabrication, manipulation, advertising, and propaganda. Similarly, Molina et al. () reviewed papers that used the terms “fake news,” “misinformation,” “disinformation,” and “alternative facts” to definitionally unify related research concepts. This review was more open-ended, entailing a snowball approach until saturation was reached in terms of variety in theoretical definitions. Molina et al. identified seven types of online content under the umbrella term of “fake news”: false news, polarized content, satire, misreporting, commentary, persuasive information, and citizen journalism. The multifaceted nature of fake news is further complicated by scrutiny surrounding the appropriateness of its various operationalizations. For example, Allcott and Gentzkow () argued that satire should not constitute fake news since it is unlikely to be misconstrued as factual (but see Klein & Wueller, ), and some reviews have explicitly excluded studies that use fake news to refer to satire (Broda & Strömbäck, ).

As researchers, we can defer the issue of falsity. For example, we can, and often do, define fake news as content that has been labeled as false by professional fact-checking organizations. Under this operationalization, a claim may later turn out to be true yet still function as fake news within a study because it was designated as false at one point in time. While this approach avoids the need for researchers to adjudicate truth, it nevertheless constitutes a distinct standard of falsity—one grounded in past rather than current epistemic status. We consider it important to acknowledge this limitation despite it being unavoidable when the object of research is defined by truth status.

The broad scope of fake news is evident in the diverse forms in which it can appear online and, consequently, in psychological research. Some studies only use the text from fake news headlines (Maertens et al., ), while others include accompanying images (Modirrousta-Galian et al., ). The amount of detail presented can also differ—both the headline and the byline of fake news articles can be included (Allen et al., ), the headline and the full-text article (Pehlivanoglu et al., ), or alternatively just the headline (Pennycook et al., ; see Fig.  for more examples). Moreover, fake news stimuli can vary in terms of its source, with some studies using artificial fake news stimuli created by the researchers (Pereira et al., ) or large language models (Maertens et al., ), and others using genuine fake news stimuli circulated online (Allen et al., ; Luo et al., ; Modirrousta-Galian et al., ; Pennycook et al., ). Lastly, in addition to content, format, and source differences, studies also vary considerably in the total number and type of news stimuli they use. For example, some studies have used as few as two news stimuli (Koetke et al., ), while others have used as many as 40 (Modirrousta-Galian et al., ). Furthermore, some studies have only used fake news (Pereira et al., ), while others have used a mix of real versus fake news (Luo et al., ). All these differences may seem small, particularly when compared with typological distinctions (e.g., satire vs. propaganda), but they introduce an additional layer of variability in the operationalization and study of fake news that must be considered.

To explore potential reasons for this variability, we turn to methodological guides on conducting fake news research. Pennycook et al. () recommended that researchers modify or select the news they use to reflect what they are researching. For example, researchers interested in how participants’ political partisanship affects their responses to polarizing news will likely want to pre-test their news stimuli for political alignment, while a study using neutral stimuli may not. Researchers’ perceptions of what can influence their measurement may also change what stimuli are pre-tested for. In the cases of memory and sharing research, news plausibility is more central in memory research (Schincariol et al., ) and more peripheral in sharing research (Arin et al., ; Sun & Xie, ). Therefore, researchers that study false memories for fake news may use pilot studies to select similarly plausible articles and reduce plausibility-related confounds (as in Greene et al., ), while researchers interested in fake news sharing may focus on other aspects, such as article topic, personal/social interest, or emotional valence (Shephard et al., ; Wenjuan et al., ).

Even when researchers take care to control for confounds related to their research area, balancing scores across multiple rating scales while maintaining the intended number of stimuli can be difficult. As a case in point, Delaney et al. () set out to make a set of fake articles about Irish and German people to test in-group/out-group effects of false memories for fake news. These researchers wanted to balance their stimuli on how common and valenced (i.e., positive or negative) their stereotype-based fake news articles were, due to potential memory effects of these two traits. To that end, they ran a pilot study in which participants rated stereotypes on commonness and valence, with the aim of creating news stories based on these stereotypes that were balanced on these two dimensions. The stereotypes used in their stories, in the end, could not be balanced—when they were equally balanced on the commonness scale, Irish stereotypes were rated as both more positive and more negative than German stereotypes on either side of the valence scale, which the researchers note may have influenced their finding of higher false memories for Irish news overall. Indeed, while Pennycook et al. () recommended using multiple scales to balance articles, they also only matched their news on a single scale (political partisanship) despite collecting ratings for 11 other scales. Including multiple scales is difficult to balance while maintaining stimuli numbers: as an example, we followed this procedure, selecting stimuli with similar ratings on three different scales using both a smaller and larger set of stimuli, and found that closely matching three scales vastly reduced our usable stimuli to ~ 25% of the rated set (this example can be found at https://osf.io/bqac5/?view_only=e8a7d8c177104bec9da7d6899c8b6678).

Furthermore, the presentation of fake news can also vary based on the research question. On the one hand, if you are interested in social aspects of fake news (such as propensity to “Like,” “Share,” or “Comment” on social media, as in MacFarlane et al., , and Yan & Pan, ) or source effects (such as reduced sharing of fakes from alternative versus mainstream sources; Di Domenico et al., ), you may choose to present your news in an ecologically valid way. Usually, this entails showing the story as if it were a post on social media or an article on a news website (Crum et al., ; Jagayat & Choma, ). On the other hand, if you are solely interested in the content of fake news, you may be wary of the “ecologically valid” presentation style, since participants self-report using context clues (e.g., URL, media company, or author attributions) in news evaluations (Baum & Rahman, ; Shen et al., ). In this case, you may choose to present news in a source-less, “sterile” way, and forgo the trappings of social media or indications of source altogether (Murphy et al., ).

These differences in stimuli, while understandable given the diverse research aims across the field, may present some problems for generalizability. For example, if an effect is found where pre-warnings consistently reduce sharing of political and COVID-19 fake news (as in Pennycook & Rand, ), can we assume this same effect will be found in more politically neutral contexts like celebrity fake news? Lawson et al. () presented combined stimuli for COVID-19 and celebrity fake news, finding that people shared the news more often when an online social connection shared the news as well; notably, they interpreted this effect for the full set of stimuli rather than separating COVID-19 from celebrity news. Qualitative research on sharing behavior has found a large variety in reasons for sharing, even when people knew the news was fake (e.g., because it was funny or entertaining; Duffy et al., ; Perach et al., ); therefore, evaluating sharing behavior by combining COVID-19 and celebrity news could mask item-specific or content-specific sharing rates. In their supplemental material, Lawson et al. () conducted an analysis on the sharing rates between the two types of news and found that COVID-19 news was shared at a significantly higher rate than celebrity news, while sharing of fake celebrity news was related to increased social perception of other sharers. It may be that the danger and health risks of COVID increased overall sharing, while some people also shared celebrity news for social reasons. In such a multivariate and convoluted research area, it may therefore be of interest to delineate findings for certain types or content areas of fake news, rather than generalizing these effects to all fake news.

Differences in stimuli may also affect replicability of fake news intervention research; interventions that work in one context may not work in others. For example, “inoculation” interventions—where participants are exposed to disinformation strategies or messaging styles to create an a priori “immune response” to subsequent viewing of fake news—seem to be robust in certain contexts for reducing sharing of fakes (for health, financial, and political misinformation; McPhedran et al., ), but reduced sharing of COVID-19 misinformation in only four of six studies in a systematic review of interventions (Smith et al., ). In these cases, the content of the news stories may be a factor in how or why people interact with, share, or believe news. Furthermore, across five studies, Wang et al. () found that when fake news articles were not specifically designed to use the techniques shown during inoculation (as they are in most prominent inoculation research; Roozenbeek et al., , ), the intervention was not effective.

Fake news research also faces multiple challenges in terms of relevance, replicability, and availability of stimuli. The uniquely short-lived nature of news media means that once-relevant content may quickly be considered outdated (e.g., research conducted with COVID-19 as a misinformation topic has dwindled since 2020; Ciupitu-Plath et al., ). At this point, 5 years after the 2020 lockdown, would using the same stimuli as these 2020 studies even be valid replication, considering how distanced from personal relevance and current news these fake articles are? Furthermore, even if researchers wanted to use the same stimuli, they may not be available. In a scoping review of ethical conduct and open science in misinformation research, Greene et al. () found that around only 37% (of 346 total) of misinformation papers across all disciplines detailed their research items (including stimuli). If stimuli are not well described or openly accessible, their content and framing are open to interpretation in replications, which may lead to further differences in the form fake news stimuli take.

Indeed, message stimuli, such as the number of likes on a fake news post, may influence belief in fake news, but diverse presentation framing characteristics makes generalizing findings difficult (Bryanov & Vziatsheva, ). In a scoping review of fake news research topics (belief, behavioral intent, etc.), Murphy et al. () reported differences in stimuli, where politics and COVID (post-2020) were the most frequently researched topics, text or text-with-image news stories were the most frequently used formats, and general news or contextless news were the most popular framings in research. Additionally, 45% of studies used both real and fake news, and only 7% of studies reported the ability to discern real news from fake news (Murphy et al., ). This review provides information about how fake news stimuli are used in research broadly across all disciplines. However, the framing and format of stimuli may be discipline-specific. For example, computer scientists training an artificial intelligence (AI) model in fake news detection may be scraping the web for hundreds of articles to use as training stimuli, communication researchers may be evaluating responses to specific fake news shared online, and psychology researchers may be using a researcher-fabricated set of normed emotional stimuli.

The presence of many forms of fake news in psychological research raises an important concern about the comparability and generalizability of findings across the scientific literature. When operationalizations and stimuli differ so widely, two studies claiming to investigate fake news may vary significantly. In order to assess the specific breadth of fake news operationalization within one subject area and to keep the review scope manageable, we have focused solely on psychological research. This allows for subject-specific insight into how psychology and behavioral research approaches the topic of fake news, though the multidisciplinary nature of this research topic still includes many different disciplines alongside psychology. Stimuli inconsistency complicates synthesis across studies and may be contributing to emerging issues with replicability in the field, such as failure to reproduce the effects of well-known interventions when tested with different stimuli or in more ecologically valid settings (Modirrousta-Galian et al., ; Wang et al., ). Addressing the issue of inconsistent operationalizations of fake news in psychological research requires a clear understanding of its scope. To this end, we conducted a systematic scoping review of fake news stimuli used in psychological research over an 11-year period (2014–2025).

We detail all review processes and exclusions, and provide all material needed to replicate this review. This systematic scoping review follows the PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic reviews and Meta-Analyses) procedure for conducting and reporting a systematic review, with extension for scoping review (Tricco et al., ). All search Boolean, list of retained papers, and extraction notes are available on OSF at the following link: https://osf.io/bqac5/?view_only=e8a7d8c177104bec9da7d6899c8b6678. This review was not pre-registered.

Then, as part of a larger systematic scoping review of fake news research methodology using similar methods to the initial pilot scoping review, a second database search was conducted using PRISMA systematic review guidelines (with extension for scoping review).

