Researchers can either find real instances of fake news or they can generate their own. To evaluate how frequently either of these practices have been used in psychology research, any reported stimuli source or generation technique was noted in the extraction (Table ). Studies that did not clearly report the source of their stimuli (i.e., where the stimuli were selected from or how they were created) were left blank (334 studies, 37.9%). Supplemental materials provided by extracted papers were also searched for source description. The highest frequency of studies (210, 23.8%) used stimuli found on fact-checking sites, of which 119 (13.5%) specified Snopes.com, 37 (4.2%) specified PolitiFact and 24 (2.7%) specified Factcheck.org. News sites were used as a stimuli source in 127 (14.4%). A total of 125 studies (14.2%) used fake news stimuli that the researchers created themselves. Some studies used stimuli from prior research (112, 12.7%), with 35 studies (4.0%) specifically citing a publication including Pennycook as first author as the source of their fake news (Pennycook & Rand, , , ; Pennycook et al., ).
