To assess the number of stimuli used in psychological fake news research, the reported amounts of real and fake news presented in each study were extracted. Note that the number of stimuli presented may be different from the total number of stimuli, as some studies presented subsets or a portion of a larger set of stimuli. A total of 862 studies (97.8%) clearly reported the number of stimuli shown to study participants. Of these, 542 (61.5%) used both real and fake news in their study, whereas 320 (36.3%) used only fake news (some of these studies showed real or fake news depending on experimental conditions, where only conditions showing fake news were extracted to keep comparisons consistent [97 studies, 11%]). Some studies used variable numbers of real and/or fake news across multiple experimental conditions, with 55 studies (6.2%) varying the number of both real and fake news, 23 studies (2.6%) varying only real news, and 3 studies (0.3%) varying only fake news. On average, 8.28 fake articles (SD = 9.48, Mdn = 6) and 6.50 real articles (SD = 10.72, Mdn = 3) were used. When looking at ratios of real versus fake news in studies that used both, 315 studies (35.8%) used an equal ratio (1:1) of real and fake news. Conversely, 71 studies (8.1%) used more real news, while 77 (8.7%) used more fake news.

Fake news in research can take many forms, ranging from an “ecologically valid” screenshot of a social media page to a “sterile” text-only news headline. To assess how frequently different presentation styles are used, descriptors of the stimuli framing were extracted from the text and noted on the extraction sheet, with common reported presentation styles categorized by the extractor. Seemingly following recommendations from Pennycook et al. (), many stimuli were presented as if they were Facebook newsfeed articles (in 205 studies, 23.3%). A further 146 studies (16.6%) mimicked other specific social media sites like Twitter, Instagram, or TikTok. Generic web news (169 studies, 19.2%) used a similar presentation of a news image and headline without any branded presentation. These can be shown alongside aspects such as likes, favorites, or “stars” to present as brandless social media. In this category, stimuli may also have actually been from Facebook or Twitter, with authors avoiding naming the platforms in text. In some studies, framing was used to present news in an ecologically valid way, while in others it was part of a manipulated condition, such as social engagement by way of likes and shares. Examples of what these three main formats look like are shown in Fig. . The fourth most frequently used format, full articles (78 studies, 8.9%), included a full multi-paragraph article or summary of a longer article. Among studies presenting only text, 286 studies (32.5%) presented plain text, and 30 studies (3.4%) presented full-text articles (or briefer full-article summaries). Other framing styles are noted in Table .Table 4Stimuli framing styles used in studiesStimuli framingn%Social media35139.8  Facebook20523.3  Twitter10011.4  Instagram222.5  Weibo91.0  WhatsApp91.0  TikTok50.6  YouTube10.1  Blog20.2Text28632.5Generic web news16919.2Full article788.9Full article (text only)303.4Video252.8Deepfake video121.4Audio60.7Vignette60.7Web page50.6Deepfake audio20.2Other3