To assess the quality and consistency of news stimuli, some studies opted to pilot a set of articles to limit potential confounds (e.g., in case varied levels of humor or emotion in news may impact believability or memory). Details of stimuli creation and any pilot studies were extracted from the text (Table ). A total of 615 studies (69.8%) did not mention or detail any pilot studies for stimuli creation in-text or in their supplemental materials. On the other hand, 266 studies (30.2%) included rated news articles on a variety of measures. Of these rated stimuli, the most common ratings included political alignment (64 studies, 7.3%), familiarity (35 studies, 4.0%), and emotion (28 studies, 3.2%). Studies using an expert selection process (30 studies, 3.4%) may have used rated stimuli but did not specify. It is important to note that rated stimuli do not always mean balanced stimuli. Some studies rated stimuli but did not use ratings in stimuli selection; occasionally, unbalanced stimuli were used in research (i.e., fake news having a lower plausibility score than real news).
