To ascertain the availability of full sets of stimuli in psychological research regarding fake news, the full text and any linked or attached supplemental material or appendices were manually searched (Table ). Most studies provided a full set of stimuli (657 studies, 74.6%). Some studies only provided a part of the verbatim stimuli (73 studies, 8.3%). That is, these studies provided the full list of stimuli but missed some part of the verbatim presentation (e.g., studies that reported using images and headlines may have only reported headlines). A total of 192 studies (21.8%) did not provide their full set of stimuli, wherein 21 (2.4%) mentioned appended or supplementary material that was either not present or available, 20 (2.3%) cited another work as the source but provided no stimuli themselves and did not clarify which ones they used, and 1 (0.1%) was available explicitly by request only.
