This review demonstrates the variety of stimuli that fall under the umbrella term of “fake news.” Across 881 psychological studies using fake news stimuli, we show that this term can cover many different content areas and presentation formats and can be pre-rated on various dimensions. Furthermore, methodological factors like stimuli sources and numbers can also vary widely. First and foremost, we consider this stimuli variability to be a strength of the literature, as the alternative, that is, using the same stimuli across studies, would severely limit generalizability to real-world settings. However, these differences—and their potential influence on interpretations—may muddle the validity of claims in fake news research. It is therefore important to improve the precision and transparency of stimuli reporting in this research area.

One potential improvement relates to the availability of stimuli. In our review, 86.2% of studies provided at least one example of their stimuli. While this is certainly a step in the right direction, full stimuli availability would constitute better open science practice. A systematic scoping review of misinformation research by Greene et al. () found that only around 50% (out of 346) of papers included supplemental material at all, and only around 35% detailed research items (like stimuli). While this indicates room for improvement regarding the availability of full stimuli sets in misinformation research, our findings showed that a higher percentage of psychology papers shared full stimuli (74.6%).

However, researchers may be wary (or legally incapable) of sharing their news stimuli due to copyright infringement. Our findings indicate that many researchers source their stimuli from fact-checking sites (like Snopes.com) or from prior published research. Therefore, any image included as part of the stimuli may fall under copyright protection. Even when researchers create their own text for fake news stimuli, they may have sourced accompanying images from actual news sources or otherwise copyrighted material. This could cause issues when sharing stimuli, as most journals require permission and copyright attribution for images, even if they fall under Creative Commons or Fair Use, both of which legally allow for use of images in research. For example, the American Psychological Association (APA, ) Publication Handbook states that copyright attributions must always accompany images published from other sources, and a letter granting permission for each image must be included when submitting a manuscript. Most major academic publishers have similar policies. Indeed, Springer Nature () lists images taken from the Internet as third-party content and advises authors to obtain written permission from rights holders as early as possible during manuscript preparation. Similarly, Elsevier () states that, unless explicitly available under an open-access license without commercial reuse restrictions or explicitly in the public domain, figures from the Internet require permission. Researchers may therefore need to start requesting copyright permission early in the publication process, and even then, permission is not guaranteed. Nevertheless, there are ways to both retain replicability and follow copyright law. News text—without images—can still be shared if their accompanying images are constrained by copyright. Similarly, especially if image content is a focus of the research, researchers could replace copyrighted images with open-access images to provide more accurate examples, though this should be clearly explained in-text. However, as previously stated, depending on the journal, this may still result in a time-consuming process of copyright permissions.

The problem of reproduceable, generalizable, and copyright-free stimuli is not new to psychological research. In research areas interested primarily in imagery and emotion, creating stimuli sets ad hoc based on the experiment often led to a lengthy piloting process beforehand and reproducibility issues afterwards, mainly due to standardization concerns (Lang et al., ). Though several image sets were created to make more reproducible and standardized stimuli (such as the International Affective Picture System; Bradley & Lang, ; Libkuman et al., ), there was still a lack of open-access material that could be published without per-image copyright attribution and permission. To this end, the Open Affective Standardized Image Set (OASIS) was created, with a main goal of creating a set of images standardized on valence and arousal while being completely available to include in publications under a Creative Commons attribution license (Kurdi et al., ). This solves several issues: researchers no longer need to take the time to create and test standardized stimuli and can share used stimuli freely (potentially only needing a single copyright attribution and permission letter). A similar collection of open-access fake news stimuli could be greatly beneficial, though the time-relevant nature of news may make this a unique challenge to address. Potentially, a resource that scrapes the web for recent false news content along with its exposure metadata (e.g., views or retweets) while using ratings (e.g., valence and arousal) to select stimuli could provide a solution for providing a standardized, up-to-date, and representative fake news stimuli pool to select from.

Regardless of copyright complications, the issue remains that the term “fake news” encompasses a wide range of operationalizations. This variability spans multiple dimensions, including content area (e.g., political vs. health-related fake news), presentation format (e.g., social-media-style vs. headline-only fake news), selection criteria (e.g., fake news chosen for plausibility vs. emotionality), and source (e.g., researcher-created vs. real fake news). Each of these dimensions could influence how people respond to fake news, so we consider it essential for researchers to (1) specify or share their news stimuli and (2) consider the implications of these stimuli. Researchers (including us) are already making efforts to do this. For example, Pfänder and Altay () consistently used the term “fact-checked false news” throughout their systematic review and meta-analysis of news judgments. They also identified the limitation that all but three studies in their meta-analysis relied on fact-checked false news; these three studies used false news scraped from the web and checked against ground truths by hired fact-checkers rather than news from fact-checking websites. Participants’ subsequent truth judgments (compared with the ground truth) yielded different results, highlighting the importance of being clear about news selection and the generalizability of findings. Modirrousta-Galian et al. () tested the effects of an inductive learning intervention on news veracity discernment. In a Constraints on Generality paragraph (Simons et al., ), the authors restated that they used fact-checked false news headlines and thus only expected their results to be reproduced with the same type of stimuli. Although few journals require a Constraints on Generality section (e.g., Journal of Experimental Psychology: General/Applied and Neuropsychology), we encourage fake news researchers to include such a paragraph regardless of journal guidelines to avoid overgeneralizations.

The importance of avoiding overgeneralizations is difficult to overstate. Consider, for example, inoculation interventions that teach people to recognize manipulation techniques thought to be common in misinformation (e.g., emotional language). Inoculation interventions have been described as psychological vaccines against misinformation and fake news in the academic literature (e.g., Biddlestone et al., ; Traberg et al., ) and popular press (e.g., van der Linden & Roozenbeek, ; Walton, ). However, as noted by Wang et al. (), the vast majority of research examining the effectiveness of inoculation interventions used synthetic, researcher-created news stimuli reflecting the manipulation techniques targeted by the intervention. When different stimuli were used, namely real-world news stimuli or researcher-created news stimuli containing other manipulation techniques not targeted by the intervention, inoculation treatments produced null effects (Modirrousta-Galian et al., ; Wang et al., ). By the time this research was published, however, inoculation interventions had already been implemented by technology companies (University of Cambridge et al., ). This case illustrates an interesting contrast to the broader literature, as inoculation research has typically relied on stimuli that were insufficiently diverse, and future studies on intervention effectiveness should focus on using diverse and real-world representative news stimuli. Nonetheless, this example underscores the importance of precise and transparent stimuli reporting without overgeneralization.

In addition to differences in operationalizations, our systematic scoping review revealed considerable variability in the number of stimuli used in psychological fake news research. We found that 61.5% of studies showed participants both real and fake news, while 36.3% only showed fake news. The design choice to use two types of stimuli versus only one type affects the measures and conclusions that can be drawn from a study. For instance, using both real and fake news allows for detection or discriminability measures to be calculated, representing the ability to distinguish between the two types of stimuli, whereas using only fake news precludes such analyses. Without real news stimuli, any improvement in fake news detection could instead reflect a general increase in skepticism. For example, if an intervention lowers veracity ratings to fake news but does not measure veracity ratings to real news, it is impossible to determine whether its effects are specific to fake news or general to all news. Failing to rule out general skepticism effects is an important limitation considering that real news is more prevalent than fake news on social media (Guay et al., ).

Relatedly, 35.8% of studies used a 1:1 ratio of real and fake news, 8.1% used a ratio that had more real news than fake news, and 8.7% used a ratio that had more fake news than real news. The ratio of real and fake news can influence participant responses. Altay et al. () found that higher proportions of fake (versus real) news headlines (17%, 33%, 50%, 66%, and 83%) decreased media trust, and while they did not affect participants’ actual ability to distinguish between real and fake news, they made participants more overconfident in their ability to do so. Orchinik et al. () exposed participants either to 80% real news headlines and 20% fake news headlines or vice versa and found that participants were worse at detecting the less prevalent type of news. That is, participants exposed to a higher prevalence of real news were worse at detecting fake news, and participants exposed to a higher prevalence of fake news were worse at detecting real news. A focus on number and ratio of real and fake news is also important for generalizability with the real media landscape, where Allen et al. () found that fake news consumption may be lower than 1% of total consumed news media. Given the impact of design choices pertaining to the number of news stimuli used, it is important for researchers to justify their decisions and discuss their potential implications.

In this systematic scoping review, we found that the term “fake news” in psychological research is used to describe a wide variety of stimuli that differ in source, topic, and framing. We also found substantial variability in both the number of fake news stimuli used and the procedures employed to select them. While most studies provided full stimuli sets (74.6%), examples of stimuli (90.1%), or framing specifications (96.8%), there is still room for improvement in replication practices regarding stimuli. However, lack of cohesion between open science practices and copyright law may be acting as a barrier to transparency. To circumvent this barrier, we recommend that researchers secure copyright permissions early in the publication process, use open-access stimuli, or provide text-only descriptions of stimuli. Another possible solution is to create an open-access standardized news database like those used in affective imagery research. We also recommend that researchers consider how their choice of stimuli might shape the interpretations and generalizability of their results. Ultimately, the inconsistent operationalization of fake news in psychological research underscores the need for greater transparency around stimuli selection, which is crucial for the reliability and reproducibility of findings in the field.

All materials and extraction notes are available at https://osf.io/bqac5/?view_only=e8a7d8c177104bec9da7d6899c8b6678. This review was not preregistered.

