Another limitation of deep learning-based physiological modeling is the black-box nature of these models, which can hinder biological interpretation and clinical adoption. For GNNs, several post-hoc explainability methods have been popularly used, including GNNExplainer, PGExplainer, and PGMExplainer [–]. However, these approaches have primarily been developed for classification tasks and are not well-suited for modeling the continuous spatiotemporal processes in tau progression. This highlights the need to develop GNN interpretability methods tailored to tau progression modeling.

In this paper, we develop an interpretable GNN framework that forecasts the longitudinal changes in regional tau burden from baseline to follow-up imaging timepoints. We compare the mechanistic FKPP- and ESM-regularized GNN models against vanilla GNNs to evaluate the effectiveness of FKPP and ESM as physics priors for learning tau spread dynamics. A key aspect of our approach is the incorporation of these mechanistic models as regularizers within a latent space. Rather than enforcing constraints directly in the observed feature space, latent-space regularization enables the model to learn underlying propagation dynamics without over-constraining feature representations. This is particularly important, as feature-space regularization can impose both feature-level and parameter-level constraints that may not generalize across subjects [, ]. In contrast, latent space regularization allows for more flexible learning of shared dynamics while maintaining consistency with known biological processes. We also develop an interpretation approach that builds on the integrated gradients (IG) framework []. The specific technical contributions of our work are as follows:
•We introduce a GNN framework that predicts longitudinal tau accumulation and can incorporate popular mechanistic models like FKPP and ESM as regularizers.•We demonstrate that latent-space regularization can reduce sensitivity to hyperparameter settings in the physics models.•We implement the IG framework that aggregates node and edge contributions enabling interpretability.

We introduce a GNN framework that predicts longitudinal tau accumulation and can incorporate popular mechanistic models like FKPP and ESM as regularizers.

We demonstrate that latent-space regularization can reduce sensitivity to hyperparameter settings in the physics models.

We implement the IG framework that aggregates node and edge contributions enabling interpretability.

In section , we provide the data description and processing details, describe the graph construction process, and present the interpretable physics-informed GNN framework. In section , we present our primary findings, including quantitative evaluations of performance and subject-level visualizations across models. In section , we discuss the strengths and limitations of our work. Finally, in section , we summarize our findings and present future directions.

