We present a graph learning framework to model longitudinal tau propagation. This framework operates at the node level and predicts changes in regional tau burden between baseline and follow-up scans. Rather than predicting absolute SUVR values of the follow-up scan, we predict the dSUVR, as longitudinal changes are subtle and more directly reflect disease progression. The framework consists of three jointly trained graph-based components: an encoder, a core, and a decoder. We formulate learning propagation dynamics in the latent space because applying physics-based priors directly in the feature space can lead to instability and sensitivity to the parameter choices for the priors. By mapping regional tau measurements into a learned latent space, the model can operate with fixed, population-level physics parameters, since the latent representation absorbs subject-level variation through the encoder. This circumvents per-subject parameter estimation and can yield more stable, identifiable regularization across the cohort. In this latent framework, the baseline regional tau SUVR vector \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
