We evaluate the proposed framework along three key dimensions: (1) the impact of physics-informed regularization on predictive performance, (2) the sensitivity of model performance to the choice and application domain of the parameters in the physics-based priors, and (3) the extent to which different physical models influence the learned representations of tau propagation. To ensure fair comparison, all models were trained and evaluated using identical subject-wise splits. We first analyze overall performance improvements due to regularization, followed by a study of parameter sensitivity, and finally examine subject-level predictions and interpretability to characterize model behavior.

We first evaluate the impact of incorporating physics-based regularization on the prediction of tau dSUVR. Performance was assessed on the test set using standard regression metrics, root mean square error (RMSE), normalized root mean square error (NRMSE), mean absolute percentage error (MAPE), coefficient of determination (RR2), and peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR). This evaluation was conducted both across all 66 ROIs as well as a subset of 18 clinically relevant ROIs termed critical ROIs (namely the posterior cingulate, insula, superior temporal, middle temporal, inferior temporal, fusiform, entorhinal, hippocampus, and amygdala across both hemispheres). As summarized in table , both regularized models ESM and FKPP improved mean prediction performance over the unregularized baseline No Reg. In particular, FKPP achieved the lowest error (RMSE, NRMSE, and MAPE) and highest goodness-of-fit (RR2 and PSNR), indicating superior accuracy in predicting longitudinal tau dSUVR. ESM also demonstrated improvements over No Reg, though with slightly lower performance compared to FKPP. These trends suggest that physics-informed regularization aids the generalization ability of the model to capture tau propagation dynamics. We additionally compared the GNN models with standalone ODE FKPP and ESM mechanistic models, which showed lower predictive performance than the corresponding physics-informed GNNs across the evaluated metrics. Each regularized GNN model was compared with No Reg and the corresponding ODE counterparts for statistical significance, using the Wilcoxon signed-rank test and corrected with the Benjamini–Hochberg procedure to obtain the pp-value. The prediction results were found to be statistically significant (p < 0.05p<0.05), as reported in the supplementary table S1. These findings suggest that incorporating the mechanistic priors as soft constraints within a data-driven GNN framework provides improved predictive performance compared with using the mechanistic models alone.

