To further assess the performance of the physics-informed regularizers on subject-level predictions, we visualized representative coronal slices comparing observed and predicted dSUVR for No Reg, ESM and FKPP, along with their corresponding error maps in figure . The error maps show the signed difference between the predicted and observed dSUVR (predicted minus observed), with positive values indicating overestimation and negative values indicating underestimation. Importantly, the coronal slice chosen captures all the aforementioned critical ROIs. Both regularized models reduced prediction error relative to the unregularized baseline. FKPP displayed more localized and sharper agreement with the ground truth, while ESM produced comparatively smoother error patterns.

These findings demonstrate that incorporating physics-informed constraints not only improves predictive accuracy but also enhances the model’s ability to capture biologically relevant ROIs implicated in tau progression.

