Having evaluated predictive performance and parameter sensitivity, we next examine the interpretability of the learned models to identify the features driving their predictions of tau progression. Specifically, we aim to determine which ROIs and inter-ROI connections contribute most to the predicted changes in tau burden. To this end, IG is applied as a post-hoc attribution method to each trained model, assigning importance scores to both node and edge features. To obtain robust estimates across subjects, we adopt a ranking-based aggregation strategy of the attribution scores. For each subject, the top-10 most important nodes and top-50 most important edges are selected based on the IG attribution scores. Node importance is defined as the frequency with which each ROI appears among the top-ranked nodes across subjects, while edge importance is defined as the frequency with which each edge appears among the top-ranked edges. In the absence of ground-truth attribution, node importance was evaluated with respect to the critical ROIs, in order to check if the attribution scores align with biologically relevant ROIs for tau progression. For edges, the top-ranked connections were categorized as critical–critical (C-C), critical-non-critical (C-NC), or non-critical-non-critical (NC-NC). Based on the hypothesis that tau propagation occurs preferentially within strongly connected and vulnerable ROIs, the attribution over node and edge features for the No Reg, ESM, and FKPP models were compared.

Table  compares the node- and edge- level attribution scores derived by applying IG on the trained models. Both ESM and FKPP demonstrated improved identification of critical ROIs compared to No Reg, with FKPP achieving the highest mean attribution to critical ROIs. Several of the critical ROIs are implicated in early tau accumulation in AD, indicating that physics-informed regularization encourages the model to focus on biologically relevant ROIs.

