These trends are qualitatively illustrated in figure . Both FKPP and ESM exhibited increased attribution to critical ROIs, whereas No Reg shows a more diffuse distribution of importance across the full set of ROIs. Several of the highly attributed regions in ESM- and FKPP-regularized GNN models, particularly the hippocampus, amygdala, entorhinal cortex, fusiform, and inferior and middle temporal regions, have been implicated in early tau accumulation in AD, supporting the biological relevance of the attribution patterns [, ]. Notably, FKPP exhibited a substantial increase in C-C connections, indicating stronger localization of propagation within biologically relevant ROIs. This behavior is consistent with diffusion-driven dynamics, which promote clustering around regions of elevated tau burden. In comparison, ESM placed emphasis on NC-NC connections, indicating a more distributed attribution pattern across the connectome. This is consistent with its prion-like propagation that distributes propagation signal broadly across the connectome, including regions of lower tau burden. In contrast, No Reg showed a predominance of C-NC connections, suggesting a more diffuse and less structured propagation pattern. In addition, No Reg displayed greater asymmetry in edge importance, while FKPP and ESM exhibit more symmetric connectivity patterns, assigning comparable importance to corresponding ROIs across hemispheres.

In this work, we presented an interpretable GNN framework for modeling longitudinal tau propagation in AD. The framework incorporates physics-based formulations of tau propagation as regularization, which guides the predictions toward biologically meaningful dynamics. The key findings from our results demonstrate that physics-informed regularization improves model performance at predicting tau accumulation, with FKPP achieving the strongest performance and ESM exhibiting complementary but less spatially localized behavior. Furthermore, our post-hoc interpretability analysis using IG reveals that using physics-based priors encourages the model to focus on biologically relevant ROIs implicated in early tau propagation. Together, these findings highlight the value of integrating physics-based formulations of tau progression as priors with data-driven approaches for modeling longitudinal tau propagation.

Our findings are consistent with previous work applying both FKPP and ESM to model tau propagation. The FKPP formulation promotes diffusion-driven propagation with local growth [–], and has been applied to regularize predictions of tau propagation []. Its application in our framework enables the model to emphasize ROIs with elevated tau burden that lie along high-gradient pathways of the structural connectome. The FKPP-regularized model captures spatially localized and clustered patterns of tau pathology, consistent with its formulation as a reaction-diffusion process that enforces local growth and diffusion. This is supported by our findings wherein FKPP captures the critical ROIs residing primarily in the limbic and temporal lobes of the brain and identifies connections concentrated within this sub-network. ESM, derived from prion-like spreading mechanisms where tau is modeled analogously to an infectious agent with clearance processes [, ], treats propagation as a global process along all connected pathways. This results in a broader distribution of propagation signal across the connectome, assigning non-trivial weight to a wider set of edges including those connecting regions of lower tau burden, which explains the predominance of NC-NC connections. ESM nonetheless successfully identifies critical ROIs, indicating that the global spreading assumption successfully captures the primary tau epicenters while also encoding secondary and tertiary pathways. The unregularized model, lacking any structural or biological constraints, produces diffuse and asymmetric patterns, indicating that purely data-driven learning is insufficient to recover meaningful propagation structure in this setting. The improved predictive performance of the physics-informed models may therefore be related to their greater emphasis on biologically relevant ROIs and more structured connectivity patterns, with FKPP showing stronger C-C localization and ESM a more distributed NC-NC pattern, compared with the diffuse attribution observed for No Reg.

The symmetric connectivity patterns and concentration of attribution scores within critical ROIs in the regularized models align with known bilateral and regionally structured progression of tau pathology. Together, these findings support the hypothesis that incorporating physics-based priors enhances not only predictive performance but also the biological plausibility of learned representations.

