On the framework side, the parameters of the FKPP and ESM models were set empirically. Although attempts were made to learn these parameters directly during model training, optimization tended toward trivial solutions which effectively diminished the influence of the regularization term. While the latent space formulation allows the model to learn relative relationships between parameters (diffusion versus growth for FKPP and infection versus clearance for ESM), these simplified models still approximate highly complex and subject-specific biological processes. Additionally, FKPP and ESM treat regional tau burden as a single aggregate quantity and do not explicitly represent molecular aggregation processes such as nucleation, oligomerization, elongation, and conformational conversion. More detailed models that distinguish monomeric, oligomeric, and fibrillar tau species were not evaluated because regional tau PET SUVR reflects aggregate tau burden but does not resolve the concentrations of these specific molecular species [–].

Finally, while IG enables attribution in the input space, interpretability remains challenging due to the accumulation of nonlinear transformations across the network. Additionally, attribution methods are not directly sensitive to the loss function, meaning that the influence of the physics-based regularization is captured only indirectly through learned weights.

